    "project_developer_name": null,
    "project_description": "This REDD+ project, titled “Reducing Emissions from Deforestation Due to Slash-and-Burn Corn by Introducing Cacao Farming in Gorontalo Province, Indonesia” is located in Gorontalo Province on northern Sulawesi Island in Indonesia. The Project Area is 43,014 ha of forest area in Gorontalo Province, consisting of dry land primary forest and dry land secondary forest. The area of the Reference Region for Deforestation is 185,403 ha in Gorontalo Province, consisting of primary dry land forest, and secondary dry land forest. In Gorontalo Province on Sulawesi Island, food for life and sources of income are dependent on corn crops, coconut palm, cacao, and forest timber and non-timber product extraction. Commercial opportunities for local communities are limited by the unsuitable low purchase prices practiced by corn intermediates. In the last two decades, deforestation in Gorontalo Province has been observed at a rate of 0.65% per year. The “slash-and-burn” farming of corn is known to be the main factor contributing to deforestation in Gorontalo Province. In short, the main driver of deforestation is the socio-economic condition in this area. Decreasing deforestation will be achieved through a series of activities that are designed to improve the livelihoods of local people. The principal project activity is introducing improved cacao production as an alternative source of income to the slash-and-burn farming of corn. The Project is community-based and consists of the implementation of activities in conjunction with local people. The implementation of the management structure and activities addressing deforestation drivers began on, as well partially after, 2020, together with associated investments. These investments are related to field work, capacities and other related work provided by the project proponent, as well as management work and conservation work such as capacity building, reporting and control activities.",
